  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'What is exact-ref model execution in Traverse?',
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'Exact-ref model execution is how Traverse runs a WASM model package that an app has pinned by digest and signature — never by a naked URL. Discover → execute → trace. One shared runtime.wasm; embedders are clients. As of Traverse v0.15.0, signed exact-ref packages run on native Rust, the browser, Swift (swift-host-v0.15.0), Kotlin/Android (Maven Central 0.15.0), and .NET (nuget.org TraverseEmbedder 0.15.0). Every host enforces model rights: model_usage must be declared or the call fails closed. Third-party ONNX models can be packaged, signed, and verified with traverse-cli model. Apple hosts can opt in to a signed Core ML variant; the default placement stays wasm-cpu. Evidence so far is macOS cargo test plus the Decision 113 iPhone spike. Governed-path iPhone evidence landed on main after v0.15.0 (traverse#1630): BirdNET on one iPhone 14 Pro Max ran at p95 141 ms per 3 s clip under the policy [coreml, wasm-cpu]. It relies on the unreleased manifest schema 2.3.0 score_transform, so v0.15.0 alone does not reproduce it. No host is Certified. digits-mlp-1.0.0 uses a test-only key (production custody is #1567). First landed in v0.12.0.',
    },
  }],
});

