---
<QuestionLayout
  title="What is exact-ref model execution in Traverse?"
  description="Discover → execute → trace on one shared runtime.wasm. v0.15.0 runs signed exact-ref models on every published host, enforces model_usage, and offers opt-in Core ML on Apple. No host is Certified."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/what-is-exact-ref-model-execution.html"
  category="Runtime and Architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>Most model stacks treat a URL or a vendor SDK as identity: fetch whatever is at that path and hope the bytes match what you tested. Exact-ref model execution is the opposite habit. The app names a specific model package — digest, signature, sidecar metadata — and the runtime either runs <em>that</em> package or it fails closed. A naked URL is never the name of the thing that ran.</p>

  <h2>Pin the package, not a path</h2>
  <p>Traverse app manifests declare exact model references. Since <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/v0.14.0">v0.14.0</a>, manifests use schema <code>2.0.0</code> and every package ships a detached Ed25519 <code>model.sig.json</code> over the exact <code>model.manifest.json</code> bytes. <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/v0.15.0">v0.15.0</a> still verifies schema <code>2.0.0</code>, and also <code>2.1.0</code> (<code>rights.derivation</code>) and <code>2.2.0</code> (accelerator variants). Packages signed for v0.14.0 keep working. An application's <code>exact_model_dependencies</code> pin binds the SHA-256 of those manifest bytes and declares <code>target</code>, expected <code>rights</code>, and an optional signer <code>key_id</code>. You cannot upgrade a model by swapping the file behind a URL.</p>

  <h2>Host-owned trust, and declared usage</h2>
  <p>Trust roots are host-owned (<code>TrustedModelKeys</code> on Rust, <code>trustedPublicKeysHex</code> on web and Swift <code>ExactModelHost</code>). Apps can never add trust. Packages enter the cache only through <code>register_package</code> / <code>registerPackage</code>, which verifies the signature and trusted key, the digest against exactly one pin, and the manifest schema (unknown fields fail closed), rights, target, and limits. Every execute re-hashes the cached bytes.</p>
  <p><strong>Breaking in v0.15.0:</strong> an app with <code>exact_model_dependencies</code> must declare <code>model_usage</code> as <code>commercial</code> or <code>non_commercial</code>. Rust sets <code>ExactModelHostConnector::model_usage</code>, the web host takes <code>modelUsage</code>, and Swift takes <code>modelUsage:</code> with no default. Without it, registration and execution fail closed with <code>usage_undeclared</code>. A <code>commercial</code> usage rejects packages whose rights say <code>prohibited</code> (<code>rights_policy_denied</code>). A host-owned package-status map blocks <code>revoked</code> packages. Every result carries <code>model_evidence</code>.</p>

  <h2>One envelope, every published host</h2>
  <p>The call surface is the existing Spec 137 <code>model.execute</code> host-connector command — not a new public guest import, and not Spec 045’s candidate/LLM resolution track. Request and response are versioned. Inputs and outputs travel as opaque <code>input_ref</code> / <code>output_ref</code> handles. Embedders are clients of one shared <code>runtime.wasm</code>. They are not separate Traverse runtimes.</p>

  <h2>What landed in v0.15.0</h2>
  <p>Spec 138 first shipped in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/v0.12.0">v0.12.0</a>. <a href="/blog/traverse-0-14-0-what-changed-for-embedders.html">v0.14.0</a> added signed packages, host-owned trust, and the first trained model <code>digits-mlp-1.0.0</code> (test-only key). <strong>v0.15.0</strong> runs those signed packages on every published embedder and enforces model rights on each of them.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Native Rust</strong> and <strong>web</strong> <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.15.0</code> (<code>ExactModelBrowserHost</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Swift</strong> <code>ExactModelHost</code> on the <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/swift-host-v0.15.0"><code>swift-host-v0.15.0</code></a> xcframework. <code>Package.swift</code> on main pins the rebuild <code>swift-host-v0.15.0-1</code> (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1695">#1695</a>). The v0.15.0 tag's <code>Package.swift</code> still pointed at <code>swift-host-v0.14.0-4</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Kotlin</strong> <code>ExactModelHost</code> in Maven Central <code>com.traverse-framework:traverse-embedder</code> 0.15.0 (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1580">#1580</a>, <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1651">#1651</a> closed).</li>
    <li><strong>.NET</strong> <code>ExactModelHost</code> in nuget.org <code>TraverseEmbedder</code> 0.15.0 (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1602">#1602</a>, #1651 closed).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Third-party ONNX models can be packaged, signed, and verified with <code>traverse-cli model</code>. Guest ABI v3 snapshot reuse makes repeat inference faster: the host prepares once and restores that snapshot on later calls. This release pins <code>traverse-registry</code> at <code>=0.28.0</code>.</p>
  <p><strong>Core ML is opt-in.</strong> An Apple host can run a package's signed Core ML variant under the same policy, tolerance, and evidence rules. Without <code>placements</code>, every call stays on <code>wasm-cpu</code>. Published evidence so far is macOS <code>cargo test</code> plus the Decision 113 iPhone spike. Governed-path iPhone evidence landed on main after v0.15.0 (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1630">traverse#1630</a>): BirdNET on one iPhone 14 Pro Max ran at p95 141 ms per 3 s clip under the policy <code>[coreml, wasm-cpu]</code> (<a href="/questions/how-fast-does-a-model-run-on-iphone-with-traverse.html">details</a>). It relies on the unreleased manifest schema 2.3.0 <code>score_transform</code>, so v0.15.0 alone does not reproduce it. There is no web, Android, or desktop GPU adapter. No host is Certified under Spec 529.</p>
  <p><code>digits-mlp-1.0.0</code> is still signed with the <strong>test-only</strong> key. v0.15.0 ships key-generation tooling and an opt-in signing workflow. Custody, rotation, and revocation remain <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1567">#1567</a>, which is still open.</p>

  <h2>What it is not</h2>
  <p>It is not “the model is in charge.” It is not Spec 045. It is not a guest <code>model_invoke</code> import. It is not a Python SDK. It is not permission to treat a URL as a model identity, to call any host Certified, or to quote iPhone Core ML timing beyond the scope of the one-device #1630 evidence.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
